  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 3,
    "lesson_title": "Second-Derivative Property",
    "lesson_core_move": "Convert a second time derivative under the one-sided Laplace transform into $s^2X(s)-sx(0^-)-x'(0^-)$ for use in $s$-domain differential-equation models.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Second-Derivative Property",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Second-Derivative Property",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is a one-sided Laplace tutorial. The transform is over the causal interval $[0^-,\\infty)$, and every derivative rule must use the left-limit convention in the boundary terms.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{x(t)\\}=\\int_{0^-}^{\\infty} x(t)e^{-st}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "To build the second-derivative rule, begin with the known first-derivative identity for $x'(t)$: $\\mathcal{L}\\{x'(t)\\}=sX(s)-x(0^-)$, and apply the same one-sided rule again with $x'(t)$ as the input signal.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{x'(t)\\}=sX(s)-x(0^-)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute the first-derivative transform into the second-derivative rule and keep both boundary terms: $\\mathcal{L}\\{x''(t)\\}=s\\mathcal{L}\\{x'(t)\\}-x'(0^-)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{x''(t)\\}=s\\mathcal{L}\\{x'(t)\\}-x'(0^-)=s[sX(s)-x(0^-)]-x'(0^-)=s^2X(s)-sx(0^-)-x'(0^-)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the target move is $\\mathcal{L}\\{x''(t)\\}=s^2X(s)-sx(0^-)-x'(0^-)$. In this tutorial, the base case is the zero-condition version: if $x(0^-)=0$ and $x'(0^-)=0$, then the rule collapses to $\\mathcal{L}\\{x''(t)\\}=s^2X(s)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Second-Derivative Property",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Transforming a Second Derivative with Zero Initial Conditions",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find $\\mathcal{L}\\{x''(t)\\}$ when $x(0^-)=0$, $x'(0^-)=0$, and $\\mathcal{L}\\{x(t)\\}=X(s)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "From the previous tutorial, the cue is the second-derivative one-sided rule with boundary terms: $\\mathcal{L}\\{x''(t)\\}=s^2X(s)-sx(0^-)-x'(0^-)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{x''(t)\\}=s^2X(s)-sx(0^-)-x'(0^-)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the given initial conditions by substituting $x(0^-)=0$ and $x'(0^-)=0$ into the same formula.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{x''(t)\\}=s^2X(s)-s\\cdot 0-0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now simplify to the benchmark form $\\mathcal{L}\\{x''(t)\\}=s^2X(s)$, which is the clean zero-initial-condition baseline for later examples.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Second-Derivative Property",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Transforming a Second Derivative with a Nonzero Initial Value",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Compute $\\mathcal{L}\\{x''(t)\\}$ if $x(0^-)=5$, $x'(0^-)=0$, and $\\mathcal{L}\\{x(t)\\}=X(s)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "From the same second-derivative template, identify the cue as $\\mathcal{L}\\{x''(t)\\}=s^2X(s)-sx(0^-)-x'(0^-)$ and use the given values directly.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{x''(t)\\}=s^2X(s)-sx(0^-)-x'(0^-)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute $x(0^-)=5$ and $x'(0^-)=0$. This introduces only one nonzero correction term compared with the previous baseline example.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{x''(t)\\}=s^2X(s)-s(5)-0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the answer is $\\mathcal{L}\\{x''(t)\\}=s^2X(s)-5s$, where the position correction remains negative and multiplied by $s$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Second-Derivative Property",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Transforming a Second Derivative with a Nonzero Initial Slope",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Compute $\\mathcal{L}\\{x''(t)\\}$ when $x(0^-)=0$, $x'(0^-)=7$, and $\\mathcal{L}\\{x(t)\\}=X(s)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the same one-sided second-derivative template, now with a nonzero slope initial value: $\\mathcal{L}\\{x''(t)\\}=s^2X(s)-sx(0^-)-x'(0^-)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{x''(t)\\}=s^2X(s)-sx(0^-)-x'(0^-)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute $x(0^-)=0$ and $x'(0^-)=7$. This keeps the position correction zero and activates only the slope correction.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{x''(t)\\}=s^2X(s)-s\\cdot 0-7",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the result is $\\mathcal{L}\\{x''(t)\\}=s^2X(s)-7$, and the slope term is unmultiplied by $s$ with a negative sign.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 3,
        "lesson_title": "Second-Derivative Property",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Using the Second-Derivative Property in an s-Domain Equation",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Transform $y''(t)+3y(t)=u(t)$ into $s$-domain form for $y(0^-)=0$, $y'(0^-)=2$, and $\\mathcal{L}\\{u(t)\\}=U(s)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a differential equation containing $y''(t)$, so apply the one-sided second-derivative template while keeping all other time-domain terms in the transform domain as the equation is assembled.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{L}\\{y''(t)\\}=s^2Y(s)-sy(0^-)-y'(0^-)\\n\\mathcal{L}\\{3y(t)\\}=3Y(s)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use these substitutions in $y''(t)+3y(t)=u(t)$ and include the given forcing transform $\\mathcal{L}\\{u(t)\\}=U(s)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "s^2Y(s)-sy(0^-)-y'(0^-)+3Y(s)=U(s)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Substitute $y(0^-)=0$ and $y'(0^-)=2$ next, then simplify the equation so $Y(s)$ terms are collected together.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "(s^2+3)Y(s)-2=U(s)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the transformed equation is $(s^2+3)Y(s)-2=U(s)$, which can optionally be rearranged to $Y(s)=\\frac{U(s)+2}{s^2+3}$ if the next step is solving in the $s$-domain.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
